\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta hailed a ground cab.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Sarah hesitated when he opened the rusted door for her. \'93Let\'92s walk.\'94\
\'93Walk?\'94 Chanta rocked back on his heels as if a prize-fighter had clocked his chin. \'93Are you mad?\'94\
Sarah possibly was mad, or at least traumatised. Exploding nightclubs, rabid PAPER agents, and van crashes were a long enough laundry list to keep any therapist\'92s spin cycle running for years. After the bombing, she\'92d spent almost two days in hospital while her burns and contusions cleared up.\
The taxi was a jagged-toothed red-iron wolf waiting to eat her, Little Red Cyber Hood. She shrank away from the hungry open door. \'93I know a place near the edge of the Ghetto. It\'92s dirty and seedy, so\'85 you\'92ll love it!\'94\
\'93Let me guess.\'94 Chanta felt clever. \'93It\'92s in the square with the statue of Saint Zucker the Redeemer?\'94\
\'93Not too far from there, yes.\'94 Sarah felt less confident about her choice.\
Chanta burst out laughing. \'93That whole area is a tourist trap. There are showers and dry-cleaners in the hover-copt pad it\'92s under, so you can wash the Cloudborne stink off before you leave.\'94\
Sarah flashed a stare of hostility and embarrassment.\
Chanta was unfazed. \'93You\'92ve been choosing the places up till now. Let me take you somewhere authentic, in the heart of the Ghetto.\'94\
Sarah nodded cautiously. Chanta hailed another cab, rustier and clunkier than the one before. Sarah shuddered, resigned for once to letting Chanta lead.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 In the Memphis Mafia Bar, a waiter called Lonnie greeted Chanta with a hug, a kiss and a grope. She showed them to a centre-room booth: Chanta\'92s regular, apparently.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Sarah eyed the cocktail list. \'93Do you have a Death in a Bathroom?\'94\
Lonnie snickered, flashed an Elvi-brand, lopsided smile, and pat Sarah\'92s shoulder. \'93Yeah, but you don\'92t have to impress us here, sugar. I\'92ll get you something more your style.\'94 Her eyes twinkled\'97mostly for Chanta, Sarah suspected.\
As Lonnie left, another waitress appeared and placed a pitcher of Choke cola, a bowl of peanuts, a jar of peanut butter and deep-fried crackers on the table.\
Sarah pulled her tablet from her bag, swiping and gesturing across its surface. Chanta shot her a look with a three-storey question mark glued to it.\
\'93I\'92m scanning their security,\'94 she explained. \'93We must be careful now.\'94 Sarah made a beeline for a corner, pat the drapes, and found an ancient microphone and transmitter. In the opposite corner she discovered a crooked old camera.\
She squeaked back into the booth. \'93I think we\'92re safe.\'94\
On stage, an Elvis impersonator was singing a tune that more wholesome universes would recognise as 
\f2\i A Little Less Conversation
\f1\i0 , provoking boos and catcalls among the genuine Elvi. Security ushered the would-be Elvis off stage and out the alley door.\
\'93Sorry about that,\'94 Lonnie said, appearing with a beer for Chanta and a Blue Hawaii for Sarah. \'93Tourist venturing too far into the Ghetto. I don\'92t know why they try. Don\'92t they know it\'92s cultural appropriation?\'94\
Sarah grabbed the beer and guzzled it. Lonnie returned within seconds with a fresh one. Chanta meekly sipped the cocktail and felt his eyeballs crystallising with sugar.\
Sarah opened her purse and retrieved the disc, wrapped in tissue. She shelled it from the paper and slid it onto the table, like she was going all-in at high-stakes poker.\
\'93You brought that thing?\'94 Even Chanta could see it was grotty from lying around in his van for weeks.\
\'93What the hell 
\f2\i is
\f1\i0  this?\'94 Sarah said, pinning the disc with her finger.\
\'93How should I know?\'94 Chanta parried. Somehow, the cocktail glass ended up in his hand and poured itself down his throat. He thought he could taste bacon.\
\'93A PAPER agent wants to kill us for this!\'94\
\'93You think it\'92s my fault?\'94\
\'93No.\'94 Sarah wilted. \'93It\'92s my fault. I\'92m the one who set that psychopath on you.\'94\
\'93Hey, it\'92s okay.\'94 Chanta clumsily reached for Sarah\'92s hand. \'93I set him onto you, too. If you hadn\'92t been in my apartment when he called, you wouldn\'92t be in his sights.\'94\
\'93Tax!\'94 Sarah\'92s eyes widened with realisation. \'93You can\'92t go home!\'94\
\'93We\'92ll worry about that later.\'94 Chanta slid the disc out from under Sarah\'92s hand.\
A rainbow sheen slithered over the disc. \'93What the tax?\'94 Sarah blurted. Chanta looked stunned.\
\'93And don\'92t tell me it\'92s the light!\'94 Sarah was flustered, which frightened him. He could say nothing while she called someone she knew. \'93Johnny,\'94 she explained. \'93Tech genius.\'94\
Johnny answered. She insisted he come meet her\'85 no, he wouldn\'92t catch cooties, and yes, this bar was much cleaner than last time\'97well, tidier, or maybe it was just the lighting.\
\'93You\'92ll have to sit somewhere else,\'94 Sarah told Chanta. \'93I can\'92t let Johnny see me with a Cloudborne.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta leaned at a tiny table jammed against the wall and drank beer through clenched teeth, watching Sarah across the room with her \'93Tech genius\'94: a bald, ruddy-skinned, soft-bodied, water-eyed fop in a suit ninety percent of security would let pass. Chanta could see this guy would lick sweat from Sarah\'92s feet if she asked. Tax! She probably already was.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta sneered into his bottle. \'93Johnny,\'94 he murmured with contempt. \'93Without your old money you\'92re nothing. Just a\'85 just a Johnny, a nobody.\'94\
Jesus taxing Christ! Johnny tried to hold Sarah\'92s hand!
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::1>}